insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
($q$a2-q-500$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Windows 10/11 edition is intended for large enterprises and adds AppLocker and Credential Guard on top of Pro's features?$q$,$q$["Windows Home","Windows Pro","Windows Enterprise","Windows Pro for Workstations"]$q$::jsonb,2::jsonb,$q$Enterprise builds on Pro and adds enterprise-only security features like AppLocker (application whitelisting) and Credential Guard (isolating credential hashes), plus volume-licensing-only distribution and Long-Term Servicing Channel availability. Pro for Workstations targets high-end hardware rather than these particular security features.$q$),
($q$a2-q-501$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A workstation has 4 physical CPU sockets and 4TB of RAM installed. Which edition can actually make use of all of that hardware?$q$,$q$["Windows Pro","Windows Enterprise","Windows Pro for Workstations","Windows Home"]$q$::jsonb,2::jsonb,$q$Pro for Workstations raises the ceiling to 4 CPU sockets and 6TB of RAM (plus ReFS and persistent memory support) for high-end hardware. Pro and Enterprise both cap out at 2 CPU sockets and 2TB RAM, and Home caps far lower still.$q$),
($q$a2-q-502$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$How does Windows Education edition primarily differ from Windows Enterprise?$q$,$q$["Education is functionally similar to Enterprise but distributed through academic volume licensing rather than sold at retail","Education edition removes BitLocker and domain join to simplify student use","Education is a stripped-down version of Home with parental controls added","Education edition only runs on ARM-based devices"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Education shares Enterprise's feature set almost entirely but is licensed and distributed through academic volume-licensing or Shared Access agreements for schools, not sold at retail the way Home or Pro are.$q$),
($q$a2-q-503$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which two of the following features are available in Windows Pro but NOT in Windows Home? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["BitLocker full-disk encryption","Domain join","Windows built-in Narrator screen reader","File Explorer"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$BitLocker and the ability to join an Active Directory domain are both withheld from Home and require Pro or higher. Narrator (an accessibility feature) and File Explorer ship on every edition, including Home.$q$),
($q$a2-q-504$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What does switching a Windows 10/11 device out of S mode allow that was not possible before?$q$,$q$["Installing traditional desktop applications from outside the Microsoft Store","Increasing the maximum amount of RAM the OS can address","Enabling remote desktop hosting on a Home edition PC","Nothing changes after leaving S mode"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$S mode restricts installs to Store apps and forces a default browser, for security and performance guarantees. Switching out (a free, one-way action) lifts that restriction so any compatible desktop app can be installed; RAM ceilings and RDP hosting are separate edition properties unrelated to S mode.$q$),
($q$a2-q-505$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician needs to enable BitLocker on a laptop currently running Windows 10 Home, while keeping every installed app and file intact. What should they do?$q$,$q$["Purchase or enter a Pro product key and perform an in-place edition upgrade","Perform a clean install of Windows 10 Pro from scratch","Downgrade the laptop to Windows 8.1 first, then upgrade again","Use System Restore to switch the installed edition"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Windows supports an in-place edition upgrade to a higher edition (Home to Pro) via Settings > Activation or a product key, preserving apps, files, and settings. A clean install would erase existing data, and there is no downgrade or System-Restore-based path for changing editions.$q$),
($q$a2-q-506$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A user runs 64-bit Windows 10 Pro but needs to move to a 32-bit installation to support an old hardware driver with no 64-bit equivalent. Which method accomplishes this?$q$,$q$["An in-place upgrade that keeps the current apps","A clean install, since changing CPU architecture cannot be done through an in-place upgrade","Changing a single setting under Settings > System > About","Running the Media Creation Tool while staying logged into the current Windows session"]$q$::jsonb,1::jsonb,$q$Changing CPU architecture (64-bit to 32-bit or vice versa) is never supported through an in-place upgrade; only a clean install with different-architecture media accomplishes it, after backing up any data on the drive.$q$),
($q$a2-q-507$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which Windows servicing option removes the Microsoft Store, Cortana, and most preinstalled apps in exchange for years of stability with only security updates, aimed at embedded or kiosk-style systems?$q$,$q$["Windows 10/11 Enterprise LTSC","Windows 10/11 Home","Windows 10/11 Pro Education","Windows 10/11 in S mode"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Long-Term Servicing Channel builds of Enterprise strip out frequently-updated components and receive only security fixes for years, aimed at systems that must not change, like embedded devices and kiosks. Home, Pro Education, and S mode all still receive normal feature updates and the full app ecosystem.$q$),
($q$a2-q-508$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician wants to test an untrusted application inside a temporary, disposable desktop environment built into Windows, without installing separate virtualization software. Which feature applies, and on which editions?$q$,$q$["Windows Sandbox - available on Pro and Enterprise with virtualization enabled","Windows Sandbox - available on every edition, including Home","Credential Guard - available on Home only","Assigned Access - available on Enterprise only"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Windows Sandbox spins up a temporary, isolated desktop for testing untrusted software, but it requires Pro or Enterprise plus hardware virtualization enabled in firmware; Home does not include it, and the other named features serve unrelated purposes.$q$),
($q$a2-q-509$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for upgrading a Windows 10 Home PC to Windows 10 Pro into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Open Settings > System > Activation","Purchase a Pro license or enter a valid Pro product key","Confirm the successful upgrade under Settings > System > About","Select Change product key / Upgrade your edition of Windows"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,3,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$You open Settings > System > Activation, choose Change product key (or the option to buy Pro), purchase or enter the Pro key, and once the automatic in-place upgrade finishes, confirm the new edition under System > About.$q$),
($q$a2-q-510$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each Windows edition to the environment it is primarily intended for.$q$,$q${"left":["Windows Home","Windows Pro","Windows Enterprise","Windows Pro for Workstations"],"right":["High-end hardware needing more CPUs/RAM than standard business PCs, such as ReFS and persistent memory support","Consumer/personal PCs with no domain join or BitLocker","Small business PCs needing BitLocker, domain join, and Group Policy","Large organizations needing centralized security features like AppLocker and Credential Guard, via volume licensing"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,3,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Home targets consumer PCs without BitLocker or domain join; Pro adds those business essentials; Enterprise layers on centralized security features like AppLocker and Credential Guard for large organizations via volume licensing; and Pro for Workstations exists for hardware that exceeds what standard business desktops need.$q$),
($q$a2-q-511$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$Windows 10 Home ships with "Device Encryption" instead of full BitLocker. What is the key limitation of Device Encryption compared to BitLocker on Pro?$q$,$q$["Device Encryption only activates automatically when signed in with a Microsoft account on modern-standby hardware with a TPM, and offers none of BitLocker's granular management options","Device Encryption is actually a stronger form of encryption than BitLocker","Device Encryption requires a separate physical USB key to be inserted on every boot","Device Encryption can only protect external drives, never the system drive"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Home's Device Encryption is a simplified, automatic-only feature tied to Microsoft account sign-in and specific modern-standby/TPM hardware, lacking BitLocker's manual controls, startup PIN/key options, and ability to encrypt arbitrary additional drives.$q$),
($q$a2-q-512$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which command-line tool tests basic IP connectivity to a remote host by sending ICMP echo requests?$q$,$q$["ping","netstat","tracert","nslookup"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$ping sends ICMP echo requests and reports reply times and packet loss, confirming basic reachability. netstat shows local connections, tracert maps the hop-by-hop path, and nslookup queries DNS records.$q$),
($q$a2-q-513$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician needs to see every router hop a packet takes to reach a remote server, to find where latency is being introduced. Which command should they run?$q$,$q$["tracert","ping -t","netstat -a","ipconfig /all"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$tracert reports each intermediate hop and its round-trip time toward the destination, isolating where delay is introduced. Continuous ping shows loss/latency to the endpoint only, netstat lists local connections, and ipconfig shows local adapter configuration.$q$),
($q$a2-q-514$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which switch makes the ping command send continuous requests until manually stopped with Ctrl+C?$q$,$q$["ping -t","ping -n 1","ping -a","ping -l"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$-t pings continuously until interrupted. -n sets a specific request count, -a resolves addresses to hostnames, and -l sets the outgoing packet size.$q$),
($q$a2-q-515$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which command displays all active TCP connections and listening ports, including the owning process ID?$q$,$q$["netstat -ano","ping -a","tracert -d","ipconfig /displaydns"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$netstat -ano lists every connection and listening port without hostname resolution (-n), including the process ID (-o) that owns each one - useful for finding what is using a given port. The other commands do not show a connection/port table.$q$),
($q$a2-q-516$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A new hard drive must be partitioned and formatted from the command line during a Windows PE recovery session with no GUI available. Which tool is used?$q$,$q$["diskpart","robocopy","gpupdate","tasklist"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$diskpart is the interactive command-line disk-partitioning utility (list disk, select disk, create partition, format, assign) - the standard way to prepare a drive when Disk Management's GUI is not available, such as in WinPE.$q$),
($q$a2-q-517$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$ordering$q$,3,$q$Put these diskpart commands in the correct order to create and format a new primary partition on disk 1.$q$,$q$["format fs=ntfs quick","select disk 1","create partition primary","assign"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,0,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$You must first select the target disk, then create the partition, format it with a file system, and finally assign it a drive letter so Windows can access it.$q$),
($q$a2-q-518$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$Which command-line tool mirrors an entire folder tree to a destination, deleting files at the destination that no longer exist at the source, and can resume an interrupted copy?$q$,$q$["robocopy /mir","copy /y","xcopy /s","move"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$robocopy's /mir switch mirrors a directory tree (equivalent to /e plus removing extra destination files) and is built for large, resumable, loggable copy jobs. copy, xcopy, and move lack mirroring and robust resume/retry behavior.$q$),
($q$a2-q-519$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A Group Policy setting was just changed on a domain controller, and a workstation needs to apply it immediately rather than waiting for the normal refresh interval. What should be run on the workstation?$q$,$q$["gpupdate /force","gpresult /r","net accounts","sfc /scannow"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$gpupdate /force reapplies every Group Policy setting immediately, whether or not it changed. gpresult /r only reports currently applied policy rather than refreshing it, net accounts manages password-policy display, and sfc addresses corrupted system files.$q$),
($q$a2-q-520$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which command shows exactly which Group Policy Objects are being applied to the current user and computer, useful when a policy doesn't seem to take effect?$q$,$q$["gpresult /r","gpupdate /force","gpupdate /sync","secedit /export"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$gpresult /r (Resultant Set of Policy) reports which GPOs are applied and which were filtered out - the right diagnostic step when policy does not seem to apply. The gpupdate variants refresh policy but do not report what is currently applied.$q$),
($q$a2-q-521$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which two of the following are valid uses of the tasklist and taskkill commands together? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["tasklist to find a runaway process's PID, then taskkill /PID <pid> /F to force-close it","taskkill /IM notepad.exe /F to force-close every running instance of Notepad by name","tasklist to permanently uninstall a program","taskkill to defragment the disk"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$tasklist enumerates running processes with their PIDs, and taskkill can then terminate a specific PID or every process matching an image name (/IM), with /F forcing termination. Neither tool uninstalls software or defragments a disk.$q$),
($q$a2-q-522$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each command-line tool to what it is primarily used for.$q$,$q${"left":["robocopy","diskpart","gpresult","netstat"],"right":["Viewing active network connections and listening ports","Partitioning and formatting disks from the command line","Robustly copying or mirroring large folder trees","Reporting which Group Policy settings are actually applied"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,1,3,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$robocopy handles robust folder mirroring/copying; diskpart partitions and formats disks; gpresult reports the actual applied policy set; and netstat shows active connections and listening ports.$q$),
($q$a2-q-523$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Microsoft Management Console snap-in lets an administrator view, start, stop, and change the startup type of Windows services?$q$,$q$["services.msc","perfmon.msc","secpol.msc","taskschd.msc"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$services.msc opens the Services console, where each service's status and startup type (Automatic, Automatic (Delayed Start), Manual, or Disabled) can be managed. The others monitor performance, edit security policy, and schedule tasks.$q$),
($q$a2-q-524$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What is the practical difference between a service's Automatic and Automatic (Delayed Start) startup types?$q$,$q$["Automatic starts as early as possible during boot; Delayed Start waits until shortly after boot to reduce startup contention","Automatic only runs after a user logs on; Delayed Start runs before login","Delayed Start disables the service entirely by default","There is no functional difference between the two"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Automatic services launch during boot alongside core system services; Automatic (Delayed Start) intentionally waits until shortly after startup completes, easing resource contention for services that don't need to be immediately ready.$q$),
($q$a2-q-525$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician wants a single custom console that combines the Device Manager, Disk Management, and Event Viewer snap-ins for quick access. What Windows framework allows building this?$q$,$q$["MMC (Microsoft Management Console)","Task Scheduler","Registry Editor","Control Panel"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$MMC is the framework behind snap-in based tools like Computer Management; running mmc.exe and adding or removing snap-ins lets an administrator build and save a custom console tailored to their needs.$q$),
($q$a2-q-526$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which tool provides a categorized, read-only summary of a PC's hardware, drivers, and software environment, useful for gathering specs before a support call?$q$,$q$["System Information (msinfo32)","Task Manager","Disk Cleanup","Character Map"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$msinfo32 (System Information) compiles hardware resources, components, and software-environment details into one browsable, exportable report. Task Manager provides live process and performance monitoring, not a static summary.$q$),
($q$a2-q-527$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician suspects a specific process is generating excessive disk I/O and wants live, per-process disk read/write detail beyond what Task Manager's Performance tab shows. Which tool should they use?$q$,$q$["Resource Monitor (resmon)","Disk Cleanup","Event Viewer","System Configuration"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Resource Monitor breaks CPU, memory, disk, and network usage down per process in real time, including a Disk tab showing each process's read/write activity - more granular live detail than Task Manager provides. Disk Cleanup frees space, Event Viewer shows logs, and msconfig manages boot/startup configuration.$q$),
($q$a2-q-528$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$Which snap-in schedules a script or program to run automatically at a specific time or in response to an event, such as every user login?$q$,$q$["Task Scheduler (taskschd.msc)","Task Manager","Local Security Policy","Component Services"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Task Scheduler creates triggers (time-based or event-based) and actions (run a program or script), automating recurring or conditional tasks. Task Manager only manages processes that are already running.$q$),
($q$a2-q-529$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician needs to reset a local (non-domain) user's forgotten password and add that user to the local Administrators group on a Pro-edition PC. Which tool provides both capabilities?$q$,$q$["Local Users and Groups (lusrmgr.msc)","Credential Manager","Task Scheduler","Sync Center"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$lusrmgr.msc manages local user accounts, including resetting passwords, and local group membership, such as adding a user to Administrators - but this snap-in is not available on Windows Home.$q$),
($q$a2-q-530$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A PC boots very slowly, and a technician wants to temporarily disable several non-Microsoft startup services to test whether one is responsible, without deleting anything permanently. Which tool and tab should they use?$q$,$q$["System Configuration (msconfig), Services tab with Hide all Microsoft services checked","Task Manager, Startup tab","Disk Management","Registry Editor, HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$msconfig's Services tab lets you selectively and reversibly disable non-Microsoft services for a diagnostic boot; checking Hide all Microsoft services first prevents accidentally disabling something core to Windows. Task Manager's Startup tab covers startup programs, not services.$q$),
($q$a2-q-531$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which two of the following MMC snap-ins are NOT available on Windows 10/11 Home edition? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc)","Local Security Policy (secpol.msc)","Device Manager","Task Manager"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Group Policy Editor and Local Security Policy both require Pro or higher; Windows Home omits both consoles entirely. Device Manager and Task Manager are available on every edition, including Home.$q$),
($q$a2-q-532$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Run-box tool opens the Windows Registry for direct editing of system configuration keys?$q$,$q$["regedit","msconfig","perfmon","dxdiag"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$regedit opens the Registry Editor, exposing hives such as HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE and HKEY_CURRENT_USER for direct viewing and editing - a powerful but risky tool since incorrect edits can break Windows.$q$),
($q$a2-q-533$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$A user reports a game crashing, and a technician wants to quickly check the installed DirectX version and run a basic display/sound diagnostic. Which tool should they run?$q$,$q$["dxdiag","perfmon","Event Viewer","Task Scheduler"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$dxdiag (DirectX Diagnostic Tool) reports the installed DirectX version and runs basic display/sound device tests, flagging common driver issues - purpose-built for this kind of graphics/audio diagnostic.$q$),
($q$a2-q-534$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for building a custom MMC console containing the Disk Management and Event Viewer snap-ins into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Run mmc.exe from the Run dialog or Start menu","Save the console (.msc file) for future use","Choose Add/Remove Snap-in from the File menu","Select Disk Management and Event Viewer from the available snap-ins list and add them"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$You launch mmc.exe to get a blank console, open File > Add/Remove Snap-in to browse available snap-ins, add the ones you want, and save the resulting .msc file so it can be reopened later.$q$),
($q$a2-q-535$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Control Panel utility lets a technician view all connected printers, set a default printer, and manage a device's print queue?$q$,$q$["Devices and Printers","Programs and Features","Sync Center","Credential Manager"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Devices and Printers lists installed printers and other devices, letting you set a default printer, open its queue, and access driver/sharing properties. The other applets manage software, offline files, and saved credentials.$q$),
($q$a2-q-536$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician needs to enable the optional ".NET Framework 3.5" component, which is not installed by default. Which Control Panel path provides this?$q$,$q$["Programs and Features > Turn Windows features on or off","Devices and Printers","Network and Sharing Center","Region"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Turn Windows features on or off, found inside Programs and Features, toggles optional OS components like .NET Framework 3.5, Telnet Client, or Hyper-V, separately from the main uninstall list of third-party applications.$q$),
($q$a2-q-537$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A PC behaves erratically after a new driver install, and a technician wants to revert system files and the registry to yesterday's known-good state without touching personal files. Which Control Panel path starts this?$q$,$q$["System > System Protection > System Restore","Programs and Features > Uninstall","Network and Sharing Center > Change adapter settings","Power Options > Choose what the power button does"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$The System Protection tab, opened from the System Control Panel applet, is where System Restore is launched and configured, rolling back system files, the registry, and programs to an earlier restore point while leaving personal documents untouched.$q$),
($q$a2-q-538$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which location under System Properties > Advanced lets a technician view or edit PATH and other environment variables?$q$,$q$["Environment Variables","Startup and Recovery","Performance Options","Device Manager"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$System Properties > Advanced > Environment Variables exposes user and system PATH and other variables, commonly edited when a newly installed command-line tool isn't recognized. Startup and Recovery configures boot options and crash behavior; Performance Options tunes visual effects and virtual memory.$q$),
($q$a2-q-539$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A laptop should sleep after 10 minutes on battery but stay awake indefinitely while plugged in. Where is this configured?$q$,$q$["Power Options > Change plan settings","Devices and Printers","Ease of Access Center","Sync Center"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Power Options lets each power plan define separate timers for on-battery and plugged-in states, including when the display turns off and when the system sleeps - exactly the split behavior described.$q$),
($q$a2-q-540$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A desktop PC has Hibernate missing from its Power button menu, though the technician wants to enable it. What is the most likely explanation and fix?$q$,$q$["Hibernate is often hidden by default and is re-enabled with powercfg /hibernate on, then shown via Power Options > Choose what the power buttons do","Hibernate is impossible on desktop PCs entirely","The PC is missing too many Windows updates","The Power Options applet itself has been uninstalled"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Hibernate support exists but is sometimes disabled or hidden depending on hardware/OEM settings; powercfg /hibernate on enables the hiberfil.sys-backed feature, after which it can be shown as a Power button option from Power Options > Choose what the power buttons do > Change settings that are currently unavailable.$q$),
($q$a2-q-541$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Control Panel utility stores saved usernames and passwords for websites and network resources, viewable by the signed-in user?$q$,$q$["Credential Manager","Sync Center","Internet Options","Region"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Credential Manager keeps Windows Credentials (for network resources and domains) and Web Credentials (saved through the browser) that a user can view, edit, or remove. Sync Center manages offline-file synchronization, not credential storage.$q$),
($q$a2-q-542$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which Control Panel feature manages Offline Files, letting a laptop user keep working on network-share files while disconnected and then reconciles changes once reconnected?$q$,$q$["Sync Center","Credential Manager","AutoPlay","Indexing Options"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Sync Center manages Offline Files sync partnerships, caching network-share content locally for offline access and merging changes when connectivity returns. The other applets handle credentials, removable-media behavior, and search indexing.$q$),
($q$a2-q-543$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$An application built for a Japanese locale displays garbled text on a PC whose Windows display language is English. Which Control Panel setting addresses this?$q$,$q$["Region > Administrative > Language for non-Unicode programs","Region > Formats","Date and Time","Ease of Access Center"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Language for non-Unicode programs, under Region > Administrative, sets the system locale that older, non-Unicode-aware applications rely on to render text correctly; Formats only affects date/number/currency display, not legacy app text encoding.$q$),
($q$a2-q-544$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each Control Panel utility to the specific settings it controls.$q$,$q${"left":["Region","Folder Options (File Explorer Options)","Internet Options","User Accounts"],"right":["Whether hidden files and file extensions are shown in File Explorer","The User Account Control notification-level slider","Language for non-Unicode programs and regional formats","Proxy server settings and security zones for Internet Explorer components"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,0,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Region covers locale/format settings including the legacy non-Unicode program language; Folder Options controls File Explorer's display of hidden items and extensions; Internet Options holds proxy and security-zone settings; and User Accounts hosts the UAC slider.$q$),
($q$a2-q-545$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which two of the following settings live in Internet Options and are commonly used to troubleshoot corporate proxy or trust issues in legacy IE-based components? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["Connections tab > LAN settings (proxy server configuration)","Security tab > security zones (Trusted Sites, Restricted Sites)","Programs and Features uninstall list","Device Manager driver list"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Internet Options' Connections tab configures the proxy server used, and the Security tab manages zone-based trust settings such as Trusted or Restricted sites - both classic checkpoints for legacy IE-based network or certificate issues. The uninstall list and Device Manager belong to unrelated Control Panel areas.$q$),
($q$a2-q-546$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician needs to allow a specific internal application through the Windows firewall on all connected networks, using the legacy Control Panel interface rather than PowerShell. Which path do they use?$q$,$q$["Windows Defender Firewall > Allow an app or feature through Windows Defender Firewall","Network and Sharing Center > Change adapter settings","Devices and Printers > Add a device","Credential Manager > Windows Credentials"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$The classic Windows Defender Firewall Control Panel applet includes Allow an app or feature through Windows Defender Firewall, where an app can be checked for Private and/or Public network profiles without touching PowerShell or the Advanced Security console.$q$),
($q$a2-q-547$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$Which Control Panel applet's slider adjusts how often User Account Control prompts appear when system-level changes are made?$q$,$q$["User Accounts > Change User Account Control settings","Programs and Features","Sync Center","AutoPlay"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$User Accounts (Control Panel) includes Change User Account Control settings, a four-position slider ranging from Always notify down to Never notify, controlling how aggressively UAC prompts for elevation.$q$),
($q$a2-q-548$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for creating a USB recovery drive using the classic Control Panel Recovery applet into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Insert a USB drive with enough free space","Open Control Panel > Recovery > Create a recovery drive","Optionally choose to back up system files to the recovery drive","Let the tool format the USB drive and copy recovery files to it"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$You plug in a USB drive with sufficient capacity, open the Recovery applet and launch Create a recovery drive, decide whether to include a system-file backup, and let the wizard format the drive and copy the recovery environment onto it.$q$),
($q$a2-q-549$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Settings page lets a user set up Windows Hello face recognition or a PIN as an alternative to typing a password at sign-in?$q$,$q$["Accounts > Sign-in options","Privacy & security","Personalization","Time & language"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Accounts > Sign-in options is where Windows Hello (face, fingerprint), PIN, security key, and password sign-in methods are configured and managed.$q$),
($q$a2-q-550$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Why is a Windows Hello PIN considered more secure against remote attack than a traditional password, even though it may be shorter?$q$,$q$["The PIN is tied to the specific device's TPM and only works locally on that machine, unlike a password which can be used to sign in to that account from anywhere","PINs are always longer than passwords","PINs are stored in plaintext on Microsoft's servers for easy recovery","PINs bypass all authentication checks entirely"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A PIN is bound to the specific device via its TPM (or an equivalent protected store) and is never transmitted or usable elsewhere, so intercepting it is useless; a password authenticates the account from anywhere, making it a more valuable target if stolen.$q$),
($q$a2-q-551$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A parent wants to add a child's account to the family PC with content and screen-time restrictions managed remotely. Which Settings path starts this?$q$,$q$["Accounts > Family & other users > Add a family member","Privacy & security > App permissions","Personalization > Start","Network & Internet > VPN"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Family & other users lets you add a family member as a child account, which integrates with Microsoft Family Safety for content filtering, screen-time limits, and location sharing, manageable locally or via a linked web portal.$q$),
($q$a2-q-552$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which Settings page lets a user switch the whole OS to a dark color theme and choose an accent color?$q$,$q$["Personalization > Colors","System > Display > Night light","Ease of Access > High contrast","Privacy & security > General"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Personalization > Colors controls light/dark mode and accent color across the OS and supported apps. Night light shifts color temperature for evening eye comfort, and High contrast is an accessibility mode, not a general theme choice.$q$),
($q$a2-q-553$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Settings section provides tools like Narrator, Magnifier, and closed-caption styling, largely replacing the legacy Control Panel Ease of Access Center?$q$,$q$["Ease of Access (Accessibility) in Settings","Privacy & security","Time & language","Apps"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$The Ease of Access/Accessibility section in Settings hosts Narrator (screen reader), Magnifier, high-contrast themes, and caption styling - functionality that used to live mainly in the Control Panel's Ease of Access Center.$q$),
($q$a2-q-554$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A user with two monitors wants one display to show content mirrored on both screens rather than extending the desktop across them. Which Settings page controls this?$q$,$q$["System > Display > Multiple displays","System > Sound","Devices > Bluetooth & other devices","Personalization > Lock screen"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$System > Display includes a Multiple displays section with options like Duplicate these displays (mirror), Extend these displays, or show on only one display - exactly what is needed here.$q$),
($q$a2-q-555$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$An employee working from a coffee shop needs to connect securely to the company network over an untrusted Wi-Fi connection. Which Settings page adds that secure tunnel?$q$,$q$["Network & Internet > VPN","Network & Internet > Airplane mode","System > Display","Devices > Printers & scanners"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Network & Internet > VPN is where a VPN connection profile (server address, protocol, sign-in credentials) is added and connected, encrypting traffic over an otherwise untrusted network.$q$),
($q$a2-q-556$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which Settings page turns a laptop's own internet connection into a shareable Wi-Fi network for other nearby devices?$q$,$q$["Network & Internet > Mobile hotspot","Network & Internet > VPN","Network & Internet > Proxy","System > Projecting to this PC"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Mobile hotspot shares the PC's active internet connection over Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, or USB so other devices, such as a phone or tablet, can connect to it. VPN, Proxy, and Projecting to this PC serve entirely different purposes.$q$),
($q$a2-q-557$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician needs to configure a manual proxy server address and port so a PC routes all traffic through a corporate proxy. Where is this set in Settings, outside of Group Policy?$q$,$q$["Network & Internet > Proxy","Network & Internet > Ethernet","System > About","Apps > Default apps"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Network & Internet > Proxy holds manual proxy server settings, such as address and port, as well as automatic configuration script options - the Settings-app equivalent of the older Internet Options LAN settings dialog.$q$),
($q$a2-q-558$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Settings path resets Windows to a factory-like state, optionally keeping personal files, without needing separate installation media?$q$,$q$["Update & Security > Recovery > Reset this PC","Apps > Apps & features","System > Storage","Accounts > Sync your settings"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Update & Security > Recovery includes Reset this PC, which reinstalls Windows while optionally keeping personal files or removing everything - a built-in recovery option that needs no external installation media.$q$),
($q$a2-q-559$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which Settings page runs guided, automated fixes for common problems such as a printer, network adapter, or Windows Update failing to work correctly?$q$,$q$["Update & Security > Troubleshoot","Privacy & security > Activity history","System > Multitasking","Accounts > Access work or school"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Update & Security > Troubleshoot runs built-in troubleshooters that automatically diagnose, and often fix, common hardware and Windows Update problems, guiding the user or technician step by step.$q$),
($q$a2-q-560$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which two of the following are configured from Accounts > Access work or school in Windows Settings? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["Connecting the PC to an organization's Azure AD (Microsoft Entra ID) tenant","Joining the PC to that organization's resources for managed access (workplace join)","Setting the desktop wallpaper","Choosing the default web browser"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Access work or school is specifically for connecting a device to an organization's cloud identity or joining it for managed workplace access, separate from personal Microsoft or local accounts. Wallpaper and default-browser choices live under Personalization and Apps.$q$),
($q$a2-q-561$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A user copies several text snippets throughout the day and wants to paste from a history of recent clips, not just the last one copied. Which built-in feature and shortcut enables this?$q$,$q$["Clipboard history, enabled in Settings > System > Clipboard, opened with Win+V","Storage Sense, opened from System > Storage","Focus assist, opened from System > Notifications","Nearby sharing, opened from System > Shared experiences"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Settings > System > Clipboard has a toggle for Clipboard history; once enabled, Win+V opens a panel of recent clips, optionally synced across devices on the same Microsoft account, instead of holding only the single most recent copy.$q$),
($q$a2-q-562$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for resetting a Windows 10/11 PC while keeping personal files into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Choose Keep my files when prompted","Open Settings > Update & Security > Recovery","Click Get started under Reset this PC","Confirm and let Windows reinstall while preserving personal data"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,0,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$You open Update & Security > Recovery, click Get started under Reset this PC, choose Keep my files at the prompt rather than Remove everything, and confirm so Windows reinstalls while preserving personal data and removing apps/settings.$q$);
insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
($q$a2-f-500$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$What extra features does Windows Enterprise add on top of Pro?$q$,$q$AppLocker (application whitelisting), Credential Guard (isolates credential hashes from the OS), DirectAccess, and access to Long-Term Servicing Channel builds - features aimed at large organizations, distributed only through volume licensing rather than retail sale.$q$),
($q$a2-f-501$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$How does Windows Education edition differ from Enterprise in terms of features?$q$,$q$Functionally very similar to Enterprise, with the same security feature set, but licensed and distributed through academic volume-licensing or Shared Access agreements for schools rather than sold at retail like Home or Pro.$q$),
($q$a2-f-502$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$What extra hardware support does Windows Pro for Workstations add over standard Pro?$q$,$q$Support for up to 4 physical CPUs (versus 2), up to 6TB of RAM (versus 2TB), the ReFS file system, and persistent memory (NVDIMM-N) support - aimed at workstation-class hardware such as repurposed server boards.$q$),
($q$a2-f-503$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$What is the maximum RAM Windows 10/11 Home (64-bit) supports, and how does it compare to Pro?$q$,$q$Home tops out at 128GB of RAM on a single CPU socket; Pro, Enterprise, and Education raise that ceiling to 2TB of RAM across 2 CPU sockets on 64-bit editions.$q$),
($q$a2-f-504$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$What happens when a Windows 10/11 device is switched out of S mode?$q$,$q$The restriction to Microsoft Store apps and the forced default browser is lifted, letting the device install traditional desktop applications from any source; the switch is free but one-way - returning to S mode requires a full reinstall.$q$),
($q$a2-f-505$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$Can a Windows 10/11 Pro PC be downgraded to Home through an in-place upgrade?$q$,$q$No. In-place upgrades only move to an equal-or-higher edition, such as Home to Pro. Moving to a lower edition, or changing CPU architecture between 32-bit and 64-bit, requires backing up data and performing a clean install.$q$),
($q$a2-f-506$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$What is Windows Sandbox, and which editions can use it?$q$,$q$A built-in, disposable virtual desktop for safely testing untrusted software, discarded on close. It requires Windows 10/11 Pro or Enterprise with virtualization enabled in firmware - Windows Home does not include it.$q$),
($q$a2-f-507$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$What distinguishes Enterprise LTSC (Long-Term Servicing Channel) builds from standard Enterprise?$q$,$q$LTSC omits frequently changing components like the Microsoft Store, Cortana, and most in-box apps, and receives only security updates, not feature updates, for years - intended for stable systems like embedded devices, ATMs, and kiosks.$q$),
($q$a2-f-508$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$How does Windows Home's Device Encryption differ from BitLocker on Pro and Enterprise?$q$,$q$Device Encryption is a simplified, automatic-only feature that activates when signed in with a Microsoft account on modern-standby, TPM-equipped hardware; it lacks BitLocker's manual controls, startup PIN/key options, and ability to encrypt arbitrary additional drives.$q$),
($q$a2-f-509$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$What is Assigned Access (kiosk mode), and which editions support configuring it?$q$,$q$A feature that locks a device down to run a single app, or a small set of apps, full-screen for anyone who signs in - commonly used for public kiosks, ATMs, or digital signage. Configuring it requires Windows 10/11 Pro or higher; it is not available on Home.$q$),
($q$a2-f-510$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$What licensing channel is required to obtain Windows Enterprise or Education editions?$q$,$q$Both are distributed exclusively through volume-licensing agreements with organizations or academic institutions - neither is available as a retail, single-PC purchase the way Home and Pro are.$q$),
($q$a2-f-511$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$LTSC$q$,$q$Long-Term Servicing Channel - an Enterprise Windows build that forgoes feature updates and extra in-box apps for years of stability, receiving only security patches.$q$),
($q$a2-f-512$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$OEM$q$,$q$Original Equipment Manufacturer license - a Windows license pre-installed and tied to the specific computer it originally shipped on; unlike a retail license, it typically cannot be transferred to a different motherboard.$q$),
($q$a2-f-513$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$Explain why a company would choose Windows Pro for Workstations instead of standard Pro for a high-end engineering workstation.$q$,$q$Model answer: Standard Pro caps out at 2 CPU sockets and 2TB of RAM, which is plenty for typical business desktops but can bottleneck a workstation built with server-class hardware - multiple physical CPUs, huge RAM for simulation or rendering work, or NVMe/persistent-memory storage. Pro for Workstations raises those ceilings to 4 CPUs and 6TB RAM, adds the ReFS file system built for resiliency with very large volumes, and supports persistent memory modules directly - letting the OS actually use the extra hardware instead of leaving it idle or unsupported.$q$),
($q$a2-f-514$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.1$q$,$q$Explain why an in-place upgrade can move a PC from Windows 10 Home to Pro, but not from 32-bit Windows to 64-bit Windows.$q$,$q$Model answer: An in-place upgrade keeps the same running OS image and swaps in new licensing/feature flags plus updated files, which is exactly what an edition change is - Home and Pro share the same core files, just gated by license. Architecture is different: a 32-bit installation is built entirely from 32-bit system files and drivers that cannot simply be swapped for 64-bit equivalents while the OS is running, since the kernel and driver model themselves differ. That requires wiping the drive and installing a genuinely different 64-bit OS image from scratch - a clean install.$q$),
($q$a2-f-515$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What does the ping -t switch do differently from a plain ping command?$q$,$q$It sends continuous ICMP echo requests until manually stopped with Ctrl+C, instead of the default fixed count of four requests - useful for watching connectivity over time while troubleshooting an intermittent drop.$q$),
($q$a2-f-516$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What does tracert show that ping does not?$q$,$q$Every intermediate router hop between the source and destination, along with the round-trip time to each hop - pinpointing where along the path latency or loss is introduced, rather than just reachability to the final destination.$q$),
($q$a2-f-517$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What does netstat -ano display?$q$,$q$All active TCP/UDP connections and listening ports, showing numeric addresses (-n) instead of resolved hostnames and the owning process ID (-o) for each - useful for identifying what process has a given port open.$q$),
($q$a2-f-518$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What is diskpart used for, and when is it the go-to tool instead of Disk Management?$q$,$q$An interactive command-line utility for partitioning and formatting disks - list disk, select disk, create partition, format, assign. It is the go-to option in environments with no GUI, such as Windows PE recovery or unattended scripted deployments.$q$),
($q$a2-f-519$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What does the robocopy /mir switch do that a plain copy or xcopy does not?$q$,$q$Mirrors an entire folder tree to the destination, deleting files at the destination that no longer exist at the source, and supports resuming interrupted copies and detailed logging - built for large, reliable folder synchronization jobs.$q$),
($q$a2-f-520$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What is the difference between gpupdate /force and gpresult /r?$q$,$q$gpupdate /force reapplies every Group Policy setting immediately, whether or not it changed. gpresult /r is a read-only report showing which GPOs are currently applied, and which were filtered out - the right tool for diagnosing why policy is not taking effect.$q$),
($q$a2-f-521$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$How do tasklist and taskkill work together to stop a frozen application from the command line?$q$,$q$tasklist lists running processes with their PIDs or image names; taskkill /PID <pid> /F, or taskkill /IM <name> /F, then force-terminates the specific process or every process matching that name.$q$),
($q$a2-f-522$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What is the difference between the net user and net localgroup commands?$q$,$q$net user creates, modifies, or lists local user accounts, including resetting a password from an elevated prompt; net localgroup manages local group membership, such as adding a user to the local Administrators group.$q$),
($q$a2-f-523$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What does the shutdown /r /t 0 command do?$q$,$q$Restarts the computer immediately - /r selects restart and /t 0 sets the countdown timer to zero seconds - useful for scripting an instant remote or local restart without the usual delay.$q$),
($q$a2-f-524$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What is the difference between xcopy and robocopy for copying files?$q$,$q$xcopy is an older utility for copying files and directory trees with basic switches, such as /s for subdirectories; robocopy is its modern replacement with mirroring, retry-on-failure, multithreading, and detailed logging - preferred for any large or unreliable copy job.$q$),
($q$a2-f-525$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$What does the nslookup command do?$q$,$q$Manually queries DNS servers to resolve a hostname to an IP address, or vice versa, and shows which DNS server answered - useful for confirming whether a name-resolution problem is on the client or the DNS server itself.$q$),
($q$a2-f-526$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$BCD$q$,$q$Boot Configuration Data - the store that controls Windows boot options, rebuilt from the command line with bootrec /rebuildbcd when a system won't start.$q$),
($q$a2-f-527$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$PID$q$,$q$Process ID - the unique numeric identifier Windows assigns a running process, used by tools like tasklist to list, and taskkill /PID to terminate, a specific process.$q$),
($q$a2-f-528$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.2$q$,$q$Explain why a technician would choose robocopy over drag-and-drop copying in File Explorer for migrating a user's profile data to a new PC.$q$,$q$Model answer: Drag-and-drop in File Explorer has no resume capability - if it's interrupted partway through a large transfer, such as a network blip or a USB drive being unplugged, you often have to start over and manually figure out what already copied. robocopy tracks progress, retries failed files automatically, can mirror so the destination exactly matches the source by removing stale leftovers, preserves file attributes and permissions with the right switches, and writes a log you can review afterward - all of which matter far more once you're moving tens of thousands of files instead of a handful.$q$),
($q$a2-f-529$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What does services.msc let an administrator control about a Windows service?$q$,$q$Its current status (running or stopped) and its startup type - Automatic, Automatic (Delayed Start), Manual, or Disabled - plus recovery actions to take if the service fails.$q$),
($q$a2-f-530$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What is the practical difference between Automatic and Automatic (Delayed Start) service startup types?$q$,$q$Automatic services start as early as possible during boot; Delayed Start intentionally waits until shortly after startup completes, reducing resource contention for services that don't need to be immediately available.$q$),
($q$a2-f-531$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What is MMC, and what is Computer Management an example of?$q$,$q$Microsoft Management Console is the framework behind snap-in based admin tools; Computer Management is a pre-built MMC console bundling several snap-ins - Device Manager, Disk Management, Event Viewer, Local Users and Groups, Services, and Task Scheduler - into one window.$q$),
($q$a2-f-532$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What does msinfo32 (System Information) provide that's useful before an IT support call?$q$,$q$A categorized, exportable summary of hardware resources, installed components, and the software environment - OS version, BIOS, drivers, and running services or startup programs - a quick way to gather specs without digging through multiple tools.$q$),
($q$a2-f-533$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$How does Resource Monitor (resmon) go beyond what Task Manager's Performance tab shows?$q$,$q$It breaks CPU, memory, disk, and network usage down per individual process in real time, such as which process is generating disk read/write activity right now, offering finer-grained live detail than Task Manager's aggregate charts.$q$),
($q$a2-f-534$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What can Task Scheduler automate that Task Manager cannot?$q$,$q$Task Scheduler runs a program or script automatically based on a trigger - a specific time, system startup, user logon, or another event - unlike Task Manager, which only manages processes that are already running.$q$),
($q$a2-f-535$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What can lusrmgr.msc (Local Users and Groups) do, and on which editions is it available?$q$,$q$Create or modify local user accounts and manage local group membership, such as adding a user to Administrators. It's included in Pro, Enterprise, and Education, but not available on Windows Home.$q$),
($q$a2-f-536$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What does the msconfig Services tab's "Hide all Microsoft services" checkbox protect against?$q$,$q$Accidentally disabling a core Windows service while doing a diagnostic clean-boot test; checking it first filters the list down to third-party services only, the ones actually worth testing for conflicts.$q$),
($q$a2-f-537$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$Which two Pro-and-above MMC snap-ins manage Group Policy and local security settings, and why are they missing on Home?$q$,$q$gpedit.msc (Local Group Policy Editor) and secpol.msc (Local Security Policy). Both are omitted from Windows Home because Home has no Group Policy engine at all, consistent with its lack of domain join.$q$),
($q$a2-f-538$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What is the Registry Editor (regedit), and what risk does it carry?$q$,$q$A direct editor for the Windows Registry's hives, such as HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE and HKEY_CURRENT_USER, which store low-level system and per-user configuration. Incorrect edits can prevent Windows from booting or break applications, so backing up the relevant key first is standard practice.$q$),
($q$a2-f-539$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What does dxdiag check, and when would a technician use it?$q$,$q$The DirectX Diagnostic Tool reports the installed DirectX version and runs basic tests on display and sound devices, flagging driver problems - useful when troubleshooting game or multimedia crashes tied to graphics or audio.$q$),
($q$a2-f-540$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$What is the general workflow for building a custom MMC console?$q$,$q$Run mmc.exe for a blank console, use File > Add/Remove Snap-in to choose and add the desired snap-ins, such as Disk Management and Event Viewer, then save the result as a .msc file to reopen later.$q$),
($q$a2-f-541$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$MMC$q$,$q$Microsoft Management Console - the extensible framework hosting administrative snap-ins like Device Manager, Services, and Disk Management, either individually or combined into a custom console.$q$),
($q$a2-f-542$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$WMI$q$,$q$Windows Management Instrumentation - the underlying infrastructure many admin tools and scripts query to read system configuration and status data, such as installed software or hardware inventory.$q$),
($q$a2-f-543$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.3$q$,$q$Explain why Computer Management is more efficient for a technician than opening Device Manager, Disk Management, and Event Viewer as three separate windows.$q$,$q$Model answer: Computer Management is a single MMC console that already bundles those, and other, snap-ins into one tree view, so a technician switches between them with one click in the same window instead of hunting down and opening three separate utilities from Control Panel or the Run dialog. It also means one set of window and taskbar clutter instead of three, and the layout stays consistent every time, which matters when jumping quickly between diagnosing a driver, checking disk space, and reading an error log during the same ticket.$q$),
($q$a2-f-544$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What can you manage from the Devices and Printers Control Panel applet?$q$,$q$View all connected printers and certain devices, set a default printer, open a printer's print queue, and access device-specific properties like sharing and driver settings.$q$),
($q$a2-f-545$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What is the difference between Programs and Features' main list and its "Turn Windows features on or off" link?$q$,$q$The main list uninstalls or changes third-party, and some Microsoft, installed applications; Turn Windows features on or off toggles optional built-in Windows components, such as .NET Framework 3.5, Telnet Client, or Hyper-V, that ship with the OS but aren't enabled by default.$q$),
($q$a2-f-546$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$Where do you launch and configure System Restore from Control Panel?$q$,$q$The System applet's System Protection tab, which also lets you enable or disable protection per drive, adjust disk space used for restore points, and manually create a new restore point.$q$),
($q$a2-f-547$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What does the System Properties > Advanced > Environment Variables dialog let you edit?$q$,$q$User-specific and system-wide environment variables, such as PATH, TEMP, and custom variables - commonly edited so a newly installed command-line tool can be run from any directory without typing its full path.$q$),
($q$a2-f-548$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What does Startup and Recovery, under System Properties > Advanced, configure?$q$,$q$Which OS boots by default and for how long the boot menu displays when multiple OSes are installed, plus what happens on a system failure - for example, whether to write a memory dump and whether to automatically restart.$q$),
($q$a2-f-549$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$How does Power Options let you configure different behavior for battery versus plugged-in power?$q$,$q$Each power plan's Change plan settings page, and its Advanced settings, sets separate values for on-battery and plugged-in states, including when the display turns off, when the system sleeps, and other power-hungry settings.$q$),
($q$a2-f-550$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$Why might Hibernate be missing from the Power button options even on a PC that supports it?$q$,$q$Hibernate can be hidden or disabled by OEM settings or fast-startup configuration. Enabling it with powercfg /hibernate on, then checking the box under Power Options > Choose what the power buttons do > Change settings that are currently unavailable, restores it to the menu.$q$),
($q$a2-f-551$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What is stored in Credential Manager, and in what two categories?$q$,$q$Saved sign-in credentials the user has chosen to remember: Windows Credentials, for network shares, domains, and some apps, and Web Credentials, for websites saved through the browser - both viewable, editable, or removable from this applet.$q$),
($q$a2-f-552$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What problem does Sync Center's Offline Files feature solve?$q$,$q$It caches a copy of files from a network share on the local disk so a laptop user can keep working while disconnected, then automatically reconciles any changes once network connectivity to that share returns.$q$),
($q$a2-f-553$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What does "Language for non-Unicode programs," under Region > Administrative, actually control?$q$,$q$The system locale used by older applications that were never updated to support Unicode, so they rely on this legacy code page setting to display text, including non-Latin scripts, correctly.$q$),
($q$a2-f-554$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$Where do you toggle whether File Explorer shows hidden files and known file extensions?$q$,$q$Folder Options, called File Explorer Options in newer builds, on the View tab, which has separate checkboxes for showing hidden files and folders and for hiding extensions for known file types.$q$),
($q$a2-f-555$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What two categories of settings live under Internet Options that matter for corporate network or certificate troubleshooting?$q$,$q$The Connections tab's LAN settings, which hold proxy server configuration, and the Security tab's zone assignments - Trusted Sites, Restricted Sites, Local intranet - both classic checkpoints when legacy IE-based components can't reach a resource or trust a certificate.$q$),
($q$a2-f-556$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$How do you allow a specific application through the Windows firewall using the classic Control Panel interface?$q$,$q$Windows Defender Firewall (Control Panel) > Allow an app or feature through Windows Defender Firewall, where you check the app for Private and/or Public network profiles - no PowerShell or Advanced Security console required.$q$),
($q$a2-f-557$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What does the UAC slider in User Accounts (Control Panel) control?$q$,$q$How aggressively User Account Control prompts for confirmation before allowing changes that need administrative privileges, ranging from Always notify (most prompts) down to Never notify (fewest, least secure).$q$),
($q$a2-f-558$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What does Indexing Options control, and why would a technician adjust it?$q$,$q$Which folders and locations Windows Search indexes for fast results. A technician might add a frequently searched folder to the index, or exclude a huge folder of rarely searched files to reduce indexing overhead and disk activity.$q$),
($q$a2-f-559$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$What does the classic Control Panel AutoPlay applet configure?$q$,$q$What action Windows takes automatically when specific removable media types, such as USB drives, DVDs, or memory cards, are inserted - for example, open folder to view files, prompt for an action, or take no action - which is also a common security hardening point.$q$),
($q$a2-f-560$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$DCOM$q$,$q$Distributed Component Object Model - the technology configured through the Component Services Control Panel/MMC tool, governing how software components communicate across a network.$q$),
($q$a2-f-561$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.4$q$,$q$Explain why a technician troubleshooting "the taskbar clock shows the wrong time" should check Date and Time settings before assuming a hardware clock (CMOS battery) failure.$q$,$q$Model answer: Windows normally keeps its clock correct automatically through internet time synchronization under Date and Time > Internet Time, so a wrong clock is far more often a software-level issue - wrong time zone, sync disabled, or an unreachable time server - than dead CMOS hardware. A dying CMOS battery classically shows up as the clock resetting to a default date every time the PC is fully powered off, not just drifting while it stays on. Checking the Control Panel settings, such as time zone, sync status, and last successful sync, first is quick and free; only if the clock resets after every full power-down does CMOS battery replacement become the likely fix.$q$),
($q$a2-f-562$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What sign-in methods are configured from Accounts > Sign-in options in Settings?$q$,$q$Windows Hello face and fingerprint recognition, PIN, security key, and password, along with related options like requiring sign-in after sleep and Dynamic Lock, which auto-locks the PC when a paired phone moves out of Bluetooth range.$q$),
($q$a2-f-563$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$Why is a Windows Hello PIN tied to a specific device rather than portable like a password?$q$,$q$The PIN is protected by that device's TPM, or an equivalent software-isolated store, and is meaningless outside that hardware context, so even if intercepted it cannot be used to sign in from a different machine - unlike a password, which authenticates the account from anywhere.$q$),
($q$a2-f-564$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What does Accounts > Family & other users let a parent configure for a child's account?$q$,$q$Adding the child as a managed family member account, which links to Microsoft Family Safety for content filtering, app and game restrictions, screen-time limits, and location sharing, manageable from the family web portal.$q$),
($q$a2-f-565$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What is the difference between Personalization > Colors and System > Display > Night light?$q$,$q$Colors sets the light or dark theme and accent color used throughout the OS and supported apps; Night light instead shifts the display's color temperature warmer in the evening to reduce blue light, independent of which theme is active.$q$),
($q$a2-f-566$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What accessibility tools live under the Ease of Access (Accessibility) section of Settings?$q$,$q$Narrator, a built-in screen reader; Magnifier, for screen zoom; high-contrast themes; closed-caption styling; and options for keyboard, mouse, and eye-control accessibility - the modern home for functionality that used to live mainly in the Control Panel Ease of Access Center.$q$),
($q$a2-f-567$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What is the difference between "Duplicate" and "Extend" in System > Display > Multiple displays?$q$,$q$Duplicate mirrors the same image across every connected display; Extend spreads the desktop across them as one larger virtual workspace, letting you drag windows between screens.$q$),
($q$a2-f-568$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What does adding a VPN connection in Network & Internet > VPN actually configure?$q$,$q$A profile with the VPN provider or protocol, server address, and sign-in method needed to establish an encrypted tunnel to a remote network - commonly used so remote or public-Wi-Fi traffic routes securely to a corporate network.$q$),
($q$a2-f-569$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What does Network & Internet > Mobile hotspot let a PC do?$q$,$q$Share its active internet connection, whether Wi-Fi, Ethernet, or cellular, with other nearby devices over Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, or USB, turning the PC itself into a small wireless access point.$q$),
($q$a2-f-570$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$Where in Settings would you configure a manual proxy server address and port, without Group Policy?$q$,$q$Network & Internet > Proxy, which supports both a manual proxy entry, with address and port, and an automatic configuration script URL - the modern equivalent of the classic Internet Options LAN settings dialog.$q$),
($q$a2-f-571$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What does Update & Security > Recovery > Reset this PC provide, and what choice does it offer partway through?$q$,$q$A built-in way to reinstall Windows without separate media, with the option to Keep my files, which removes apps and settings but preserves personal data, or Remove everything, a full factory-style wipe.$q$),
($q$a2-f-572$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What is the purpose of Update & Security > Troubleshoot in Settings?$q$,$q$It runs guided, automated troubleshooters for common problems - printing, network adapters, Windows Update itself, audio, and more - diagnosing and often fixing the issue without manual command-line steps.$q$),
($q$a2-f-573$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What does Accounts > Access work or school configure, and how does it differ from a personal Microsoft account?$q$,$q$It connects the device to an organization's Azure AD (Microsoft Entra ID) tenant, or joins it to that organization's managed resources through workplace join - distinct from signing in with a personal Microsoft account, and it's how many corporate mobile-device-management policies get applied.$q$),
($q$a2-f-574$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$What does enabling Clipboard history, under System > Clipboard, change about copy and paste in Windows?$q$,$q$Instead of holding only the single most recently copied item, Win+V opens a panel listing several recent clips to choose from, and clipboard content can optionally sync across devices signed into the same Microsoft account.$q$),
($q$a2-f-575$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$IME$q$,$q$Input Method Editor - the Windows component, configured under Time & language > Language, that lets a standard keyboard input complex scripts, such as Chinese, Japanese, or Korean characters.$q$),
($q$a2-f-576$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$UWP$q$,$q$Universal Windows Platform - the modern app model for apps distributed via the Microsoft Store, sandboxed and managed largely through Settings > Apps rather than legacy Control Panel tools.$q$),
($q$a2-f-577$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.5$q$,$q$Explain why Microsoft has been steadily moving functionality out of Control Panel and into the Settings app instead of maintaining both indefinitely.$q$,$q$Model answer: Control Panel's applet-based design dates back to early Windows and doesn't adapt well to touch input, cloud-connected accounts, or the per-app granular permissions modern Windows needs, such as camera, microphone, or location access per app, or syncing settings via a Microsoft account. Settings was built as a single, consistently styled, touch-and-mouse-friendly framework that can host those newer concepts cleanly. Rather than maintaining two overlapping configuration systems forever, Microsoft has incrementally rehomed features into Settings and left Control Panel for legacy pieces that haven't been rebuilt yet, with the expectation that Control Panel keeps shrinking over time.$q$);
